WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.962 line:-1 align:center size:84%
MV AutoML Studio. From a spreadsheet
to a model that predicts, without

00:00:05.962 --> 00:00:08.260 line:-1 align:center size:84%
writing a single line of code.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.708 line:-1 align:center size:84%
You start by bringing in the data.
An Excel file, a CSV, or straight

00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:16.110 line:-1 align:center size:84%
from your server.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.550 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Drop the file and that's it: three
thousand rows read.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.830 line:-1 align:center size:84%
The platform tells you on its own
what's inside.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.300 line:-1 align:center size:84%
How much is missing, what repeats,
which column is of no use at all.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.910 line:-1 align:center size:84%
And column by column: the amounts,
the days overdue, the channel used

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.310 line:-1 align:center size:84%
to call.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.780 line:-1 align:center size:84%
It even reads the notes typed by
hand and adds them to the analysis.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.436 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Here you see what moves together
with what. The stronger the colour,

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:48.960 line:-1 align:center size:84%
the closer they move.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.320 line:-1 align:center size:84%
It also tells you what identifies
each row and whether any period is

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.720 line:-1 align:center size:84%
missing.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.240 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Now you say what you want to know,
in your own words: whether the

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.640 line:-1 align:center size:84%
customer will pay.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.410 line:-1 align:center size:84%
It understands, finds the column and
builds the plan.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.770 line:-1 align:center size:84%
It recognises it's a yes-or-no
question, and tells you how

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.170 line:-1 align:center size:84%
confident it is.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.640 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Then it prepares the data and
removes whatever already carries the

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.040 line:-1 align:center size:84%
answer inside.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.310 line:-1 align:center size:84%
It trains. It tries different
families of models and keeps the

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.710 line:-1 align:center size:84%
best one.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.270 line:-1 align:center size:84%
The result is measured on data the
model never saw. That's the number

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.670 line:-1 align:center size:84%
that counts.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.970 line:-1 align:center size:84%
The closer to one, the better it
separates those who pay from those

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.370 line:-1 align:center size:84%
who don't.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.010 line:-1 align:center size:84%
And it explains why: what weighs,
and which way it pushes.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.918 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Talking to the person is what weighs
the most. The older the debt, the

00:01:46.918 --> 00:01:48.380 line:-1 align:center size:84%
less gets collected.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.700 line:-1 align:center size:84%
Everything goes down to Excel, ready
to use.
